    "q-103295": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103295",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\nI. The vertical asymptotes of the function are $x=2n\\pi $\n\nII. Its graph passes through the point $(0,0)$\n\nIII. $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14260557/assets/c2d0606829588b01f6cba2fa4785b201c47a6d9ffa325d54e64544c9769d7e4a.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the graph of the function passes through the point $(0,0).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ has period $\\pi .$ So, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi )=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
