    "q-158040": {
      "dom_id": "step-q158040",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $\\operatorname{tan}⁡a=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-a+9\\pi )=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14260557/assets/df1aba3f5ff980de82a74dde8170ad708e379caf2188873bcddc83177e87c7a6.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-a+9\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-a+\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-a) & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-a+12\\pi )=-\\frac{5}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
